  <!-- ========== 3. 24-48H DELTA ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 12 Days: June 10–22)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive &middot; banned-verb lint enforced &middot; each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">
      <p>On June 14, Pakistan's Prime Minister Shehbaz Sharif announced that the United States and Iran had finalized the text of the Islamabad Memorandum of Understanding — a 14-point framework agreement ending active hostilities, ordering a multi-front ceasefire, and mandating Iranian cooperation on Hormuz reopening and demining. Iranian Foreign Minister Araghchi's participation as a named signatory constitutes an official rescinding of Iran's June 1 suspension of negotiations, meeting F1's resolution criteria before EOD June 16. VP Vance publicly confirmed the digital signing on June 15; Trump and President Pezeshkian signed remotely on June 17 at Versailles and Tehran respectively. The MOU does not resolve the nuclear file — enrichment caps and HEU disposal are explicitly deferred to working groups within the 60-day framework. → moved <span class="cite">F1</span>, <span class="cite">F2</span></p>

      <p>The first round of formal nuclear-track talks under the MOU framework was scheduled for June 20 at the Bürgenstock Resort in Switzerland but was canceled after Iran demanded a halt to Israeli military operations in Lebanon as a precondition. On June 20–21, Israel and Hezbollah agreed to a fresh ceasefire following intensified fighting that threatened the Swiss track; this unblocked negotiations. Witkoff, Kushner, and VP Vance arrived in Switzerland; Araghchi and Iranian parliamentary speaker Qalibaf traveled to Bürgenstock. The session convened on June 21 and concluded with a Pakistan-Qatar joint mediators' statement describing "encouraging progress" and the establishment of a High Level Committee with three working groups (nuclear, sanctions, monitoring/dispute-resolution). The opening session's cancellation and rapid reconvening within 24 hours demonstrate both Iran's use of Lebanon as active leverage and the parties' shared interest in keeping the 60-day clock running. → moved <span class="cite">F2</span></p>

      <p>On June 19, Kpler recorded approximately 20 tankers transiting Hormuz — the highest single-day figure since June 2 — following the MOU's entry into force. On June 20, CENTCOM reported 55 merchant vessels transiting, carrying more than 17 million barrels of oil. The same day, IRGC Navy declared the strait "completely closed," citing alleged Israeli violations of the MOU's Lebanon clauses. CENTCOM Captain Tim Hawkins directly disputed the declaration: "Iran does not control the Strait of Hormuz. Traffic continues to flow." Argus Media reported tanker traffic as "unchanged" relative to the CENTCOM figures, and Iran issued radio warnings to vessels. The June 20 re-closure declaration — three days after MOU signing — is the first major enforcement dispute of the new framework. The 7-day rolling average remains far below 50. → moved <span class="cite">F4</span></p>

      <p>RFA Lyme Bay completed mine-warfare training in the Mediterranean and arrived in Cyprus, loaded with autonomous minehunting systems and over 100 Royal Navy Diving and Threat Exploitation Group personnel. The UK and France formally finalized a multinational mine-clearing coalition of 15 committed countries. The EU's diplomatic service designated Operation Aspides as the bloc's primary demining contribution to the Franco-British effort. Under MOU terms, Iran committed to "best efforts for safe passage" and demining arrangements within 30 days of signing (i.e., by mid-July). However, Iran has acknowledged it cannot locate several mines that have drifted from their original positions due to currents. The June 20 IRGC re-closure declaration introduces ambiguity about whether the "permissible environment" condition for MCM vessel entry will be met before July 10. → moved <span class="cite">F5</span></p>

      <p>Inside the Knesset, no second or third reading of the coalition's dissolution bill has been reported as of June 22. The election date dispute between Netanyahu (preferring October) and Shas/UTJ (preferring September before High Holy Days) remains unresolved. No Haredi party has formally departed the coalition; no Smotrich departure reported. The Islamabad MOU introduces a new variable into Israeli domestic politics — whether a ceasefire and diplomatic framework alter the electoral calendar calculus is a question for the June 28 Sunday review. → supports <span class="cite">F6</span>, <span class="cite">F7</span> without move</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 4. SCENARIO MAP ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="scenario-map">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Scenario Map
      <span class="meta">Computed from Forecast Board &middot; 1 sentence per scenario</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="scenario-map">
      <div class="prob-bar-container">
        <div class="prob-bar prob-escalation" style="width:25%;">Escalation 25%</div>
        <div class="prob-bar prob-status" style="width:45%;">Protracted 45%</div>
        <div class="prob-bar prob-deescalation" style="width:30%;">De-esc 30%</div>
      </div>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-esc">Escalation path (25%):</strong> Iran's June 20 re-closure declaration leads to a physical IRGC obstruction of MCM vessels (N2 YES), collapsing the demining track and triggering a new military response; the Switzerland nuclear talks break down over Lebanon.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-prot">Protracted path (45%):</strong> The MOU framework holds nominally — talks continue in working groups, Hormuz transits stay in the 15–40/day range, demining advances slowly — but the 60-day deadline produces another extension rather than a final deal, with Lebanon as the recurring sticking point.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-desc">De-escalation path (30%):</strong> MCM operations commence by July 10 (F5 YES), the nuclear working group produces a framework text by late July (N1 YES), 7-day Hormuz averages approach or exceed 50, and the 60-day clock yields a final deal by mid-August.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 5. INSIDE IRAN ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode &middot; 200 words &middot; verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>IRNA and Press TV have framed the Islamabad MOU as a diplomatic "victory" for the Islamic Republic — Iran's state media uniformly emphasized the $300 billion reconstruction commitment and the US agreement to lift the naval blockade, while giving minimal coverage to the nuclear deferral language. Tasnim published Araghchi's characterization of the Bürgenstock session as "constructive," without quoting specifics on enrichment or HEU. Kayhan, which typically runs the hardline editorial line, has been notably restrained since the MOU signing — carrying Araghchi's statements without the customary oppositional commentary, a pattern consistent with Mojtaba Khamenei's written intermediaries having authorized the deal track. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
      <p>The June 20 IRGC re-closure declaration received extensive state-media treatment: IRNA and Tasnim framed it as a legitimate enforcement of MOU terms against Israeli "crimes" in Lebanon, not as a unilateral abrogation of the deal. This framing preserves the domestic narrative that Iran acted within the agreement's framework rather than against it — a distinction that creates diplomatic space for the Bürgenstock working groups to continue even as the IRGC publicly asserts Hormuz control. CENTCOM's counter-statement disputing the closure went unreported in Iranian state media. → supports <span class="cite">F4</span>, <span class="cite">F9</span> without move</p>
      <p>On the nuclear track, Iranian state media have not reported any specific enrichment-cap or HEU-disposal figures from the Switzerland session — consistent with both the "encouraging progress" framing and with the working-group structure that defers specific numbers to subsequent rounds. Hardliner outlets have not mounted a visible public campaign against the Switzerland talks as of June 22, though the parliamentary speaker Qalibaf's presence in the delegation is unusual and may be intended to pre-empt Majles opposition. → supports <span class="cite">F8</span> without move</p>
    </div>
  </div>

